 {
  "key": "indices.indices.q12",
  "type": "MULTIPLE_CHOICE",
  "concepts": [
   "amath.indices.indices"
  ],
  "difficulty": 2,
  "marks": 1,
  "estimatedSeconds": 90,
  "stem": "Using the substitution $u = 2^{x}$, which quadratic equation is equivalent to $4^{x} - 6 \\times 2^{x} + 8 = 0$?",
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "content": "$2u - 6u + 8 = 0$",
    "rationale": "$4^{x}$ is not $2 \\times 2^{x}$; it is $\\left(2^{x}\\right)^{2}$."
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "content": "$u^{2} - 6u + 8 = 0$",
    "correct": true,
    "rationale": "Correct: $4^{x} = \\left(2^{2}\\right)^{x} = \\left(2^{x}\\right)^{2} = u^{2}$."
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "content": "$u^{4} - 6u + 8 = 0$",
    "rationale": "$4^{x}$ is $u^{2}$, not $u^{4}$: only the base 4 is rewritten as $2^{2}$."
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "content": "$u^{2} - 6u^{2} + 8 = 0$",
    "rationale": "The middle term $6 \\times 2^{x}$ is $6u$, not $6u^{2}$."
   }
  ],
  "explanation": "$4^{x} = 2^{2x} = \\left(2^{x}\\right)^{2} = u^{2}$, and $6 \\times 2^{x} = 6u$, so the equation is $u^{2} - 6u + 8 = 0$ (giving $u = 2$ or $4$, so $x = 1$ or $2$).",
  "modelResponse": {
   "type": "MULTIPLE_CHOICE",
   "optionKeys": [
    "B"
   ]
  },
  "wrongResponses": [
   {
    "type": "MULTIPLE_CHOICE",
    "optionKeys": [
     "A"
    ]
   }
  ],
